{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And you.</b> The ancients said that after the death of Nebuchadnezzar he was dragged from his grave, for the people of his kingdom were saying that perhaps he had not died and would yet return as he returned the first time. This too is correct, and the truth is that this verse is about Nebuchadnezzar, for he is the beginning of the kingdom of Babylon over Israel, and the witness prepared the slaughter for his sons:" "<b>Like netzer.</b> Branch:" "<b>Pierced by the sword.</b> In the language of Kedar, like מדוקרים (the pierced). And some say from the root of טענו (Genesis 45:17) on a distant road, like 'laden.'" "<b>Trampled.</b> Pu'al from the two [letters] visible [in the root]."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {2 "Ibn Ezra cites the Kedarite (Arabic) cognate reading מטועני as 'pierced'; the alternative derives it from טען 'to load,' hence 'laden with swords.'", 3 "He explains מובס as a pu'al form whose root has only two visible radical letters (בס), i.e., a hollow/weak root."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:53:49.523539023Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}